
  [1mStorage[0m — [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, key [38;5;153mapp.session.v1[39m. The key is versioned so a future shape change is detected rather than
  misread as valid. Expiry is a 24h check against [38;5;153mloginAt[39m on every read; an expired record is cleared on the spot rather
  than left to rot.

  [1mData flow[0m — submit → [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m → [38;5;153mlogin()[39m → on success [38;5;153mAppSession.save(...)[39m. On page load, [38;5;153mapp.js[39m reads [38;5;153mgetSession()
[39m  and logs the restored user, which is what makes "persists across reloads and tabs" observable.

  [1mError handling[0m — [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m throws in Safari private mode and when quota or cookies are blocked, so every read and
  write is wrapped; on failure the module falls back to an in-memory object so the current page still works instead of
  the form dying. Malformed JSON is treated as no session.

  [1mTwo things I want on the record[0m, both going into the spec as stated assumptions:

  1. [1mThe stub userId is not real identity.[0m [38;5;153mlogin()[39m will synthesize one via [38;5;153mcrypto.randomUUID()[39m. That means a [3mdifferent[0m
     ID for the same person on each login and on each browser — it tracks a login event, not a human. Real "who logged
     in" tracking only arrives when the server supplies the ID; the swap is a one-line change inside [38;5;153mlogin()[39m and touches
     nothing else. If you'd rather the stub derive the ID from the username so it's stable while you're testing, say so
     — it's less realistic but more useful for eyeballing.
  2. [1mThis is not an auth boundary.[0m Anything in [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m is readable and editable by the user, so the persisted
     userId is for display and tracking only. The server must never trust it to decide who someone is.

  [1mTesting[0m — [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has no scripts, no test runner, and no linter. Worth settling before code exists rather than
  after:
